Gilbreath's conjecture is an open problem in number theory. It states that for the sequence of primes $p_n$, if we form the sequence of absolute differences iteratively (first differences, then absolute differences of those, etc.), then the first term of every difference sequence beyond the zeroth is 1.

Formally, with $d^0(n) = p_n$ and $d^{k+1}(n) = |d^k(n+1) - d^k(n)|$, the conjecture asserts $d^k(0) = 1$ for all $k \geq 1$.

This conjecture has been verified computationally for enormous ranges (well beyond $k$ corresponding to all primes up to very large bounds), but no proof exists. It is classified in the Lean formalization as `@[category research open]`, confirming its status as an open research problem.

